error[NK1260]: `HashMap` finds its keys by their hash, and `Name` doesn't hash.
   --> tests/a_key_that_does_not_hash_is_refused.nika:19:17
    |
 19 | struct Holder { by_name: collections::HashMap[Name, i64] }
    |                 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    |
    = note: `Name` says with its own `impl Eq` when two values are equal, so a hash made from its fields could tell two equal values apart.
    = help: Write `impl Hash for Name { … }` that feeds `into.add(…)` what `eq` compares, or key the `HashMap` by a text or a number.
error[NK1260]: `HashSet` finds its keys by their hash, and `Q` doesn't hash: its part `f64` doesn't.
   --> tests/a_key_that_does_not_hash_is_refused.nika:21:10
    |
 21 | fn count(m: collections::HashSet[Q]) -> i64 { m.len() }
    |          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    |
    = note: An integer, a `String`, a `bool`, a `scalar`, `Bytes`, a tuple, list or option of those, and a `struct` or an `enum` you declare whose parts all hash, hash. A float doesn't (`NaN` isn't equal to itself), nor does a map or a set, and a type from a library hashes only where its ledger says `hashes = true`.
    = help: Key the `HashSet` by a text or a number, or leave the part that doesn't hash out of the key.
